# JobPilot — Master Project Rules

You are a senior full-stack + AI engineer helping me build "JobPilot", a production-grade AI job-hunt assistant for my portfolio. Read this carefully; it is the permanent context for every future task.

## PRODUCT

A user uploads a resume, reviews/edits the parsed profile, gets ranked job matches with explanations and skill gaps, and on demand prepares an application for one job: company research, a tailored resume (rephrasing only, never inventing facts), a cover letter, and interview prep. They track applications on a kanban board and see analytics.

## ARCHITECTURE (monorepo)

- `apps/web`: React 18 + Vite + TypeScript, React Router, TanStack Query, Tailwind + shadcn/ui, Zustand for small UI state, react-hook-form + zod.
- `apps/api`: Node 20 + Express + TypeScript, MongoDB (Mongoose), Redis + BullMQ, zod validation, JWT auth (access token + refresh token in httpOnly cookie), helmet, express-rate-limit, pino logging. This is the ONLY service the browser talks to.
- `apps/ai-service`: Python 3.11 + FastAPI, LangGraph, Pydantic v2, sentence-transformers, LiteLLM-style provider fallback (Groq primary, Gemini fallback). Private/internal: protected by an X-Service-Key header, holds no user state, never touches MongoDB for business data.
- `packages/shared`: shared TS types and the generated OpenAPI client for the AI service.
- `docker-compose.yml` runs web, api, ai-service, mongo, redis for local dev.
- `legacy/`: my old Streamlit project. Reference only. Never import from it or edit it.

## HARD RULES

1. TypeScript strict mode everywhere in JS land; Python fully type-hinted, ruff + mypy clean.
2. Every LLM call returns a Pydantic-validated structured output with retry-on-validation-failure (max 2 retries). Never parse free text with regex.
3. Treat job descriptions, scraped pages and resume text as UNTRUSTED input: wrap in delimiters, instruct the model to treat them as data, strip HTML, cap length.
4. The tailoring and cover-letter agents must only use facts present in the user's verified profile. No invented skills, employers, metrics or dates.
5. No secrets in code. All config via env vars validated at startup (zod in api, pydantic-settings in ai-service). Model names are env vars, never hardcoded.
6. Never log resume text or other PII. Log ids, durations, token counts, cost.
7. Every endpoint has input validation, auth (except health/login/register), consistent error shape `{error:{code,message,details?}}`, and a test.
8. No auto-apply features and no scraping of LinkedIn/Indeed. Job data comes from public APIs through a JobSource adapter interface.
9. Long-running work (>3s) goes through BullMQ jobs; the browser gets progress via SSE or polling, never a hanging request.
10. Prefer small files and pure functions. Put business logic in services, not routers/controllers.

## WORKFLOW RULES

- Before coding any task, state a short plan and list the files you will create or change.
- After coding, run the linter, type-check and tests for the area you touched, and fix failures before reporting.
- Do not touch other apps than the one named in the task unless I say so.
- When a requirement is ambiguous, pick the simplest sensible default, state it in one line, and continue.
- Finish each task with: what was built, how to run it, and any follow-ups. Keep it short.
